THE FILE
   6 bytes: 4869c3a9090a

1. xxd -- one byte at a time, in file order
   00000000: 4869 c3a9 090a                           Hi....
   The middle column is the file. Nothing here was reordered, decoded or
   guessed.

2. THE TEXT COLUMN IS ALREADY A READING
   Hi.... -- FOUR of the six bytes drew as dots. c3 and a9 are one
   letter and the column shows two dots for it; 09 and 0a add two more.
   The column tests each byte against ASCII on its own, so it can never
   show you a character that took more than one byte.

3. cat -v -- the same bytes respelled in ASCII
   HiM-CM-)	
   M-C M-) is c3 a9, and ^I is the tab. Nothing is lost and nothing is
   decoded: it is a pure respelling, which is why it survives being
   pasted into an email and xxd does not.

4. WHAT THIS KEY DELIBERATELY DOES NOT RUN
   od -a  names the low bytes -- ht, nl, sp -- and the names it uses
          differ between the BSD and GNU builds. Its default is octal
          WORDS at octal offsets, which is a C type, not a file.
   hexdump  with no flags prints 16-bit words in your CPU's byte order,
          so 'Hi' comes out as 6948 on a little-endian machine. The bytes
          were reordered to suit an integer that is not in the file.
   Both are recorded nowhere in this key because their output is a fact
   about the machine. Use hexdump -C, or xxd, when you need the file.

5. file -- the only one that is guessing
   It reads the first bytes and matches a table. On this file it will say
   something about text; on a file one byte longer it might say something
   else. A guess is useful and it is not evidence.

THE RULE
   Exactly one column in this whole page is the file: the hex, printed
   one byte at a time, in file order. Everything else -- the text column,
   cat -v, od's names, file's verdict -- is a reading, and every reading
   has already decided something on your behalf.
